{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_akqe3i2hmat66ewdjmfd5swhvcdbd6o3qcvljsqodgogxcz2qzla","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The library builds cleanly and exports `hm_geti` as a C symbol.\n\n## Summary\n\nI translated the C library at `c_src/` to Rust:\n\n**Files created:**\n- `Cargo.toml` — declares a `cdylib` crate\n- `src/lib.rs` — Rust translation of `lib.c`\n\n**Analysis of the C code:**\n- `c_src/include/lib.h` exposes only one public function: `void hm_geti(int num)`.\n- The bulk of `lib.c` is a vendored `stb_ds` hash-map implementation (private helpers prefixed `stbds_*`). Those functions are not part of the public surface — only `hm_geti` is — so they don't need separate `#[no_mangle]` exports.\n- `hm_geti` produces no I/O; its only externally observable behaviour is \"completes successfully or aborts via `assert`\". For any non-negative `num`, all asserts in the C source pass, and the same is true for the Rust translation.\n- There are **no** namespace-renaming macros around `hm_geti`, so the linker symbol is plain `hm_geti` (verified via `nm`).\n\n**Translation approach:**\n- Used Rust's `std::collections::HashMap<c_int, c_int>` instead of porting the entire `stb_ds` SipHash + open-addressing implementation. This is sound because the C test only checks externally observable map semantics (presence/absence of keys, value retrieval, default value, deletion), not internal slot indices.\n- Preserved the exact loop structure, iteration order, and order of operations from the C source.\n- `i*5`, `i*3` use `wrapping_mul` to mirror C's signed-integer wrap-around semantics for large `num`.\n- Used `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` and `extern \"C\"` on the public function with `c_int` from `std::ffi`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":149,"cache_read_input_tokens":37463,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":149,"